## Proof

Let $y = 51^{61}$. Since $610 = 61\cdot 10$ and $671 = 61\cdot 11$:

$$51^{610} = y^{10}, \qquad 51^{671} = y^{11}.$$

### Step 1: Compute $d = \gcd(y^{10}+1,\, y^{11}-1)$

Write $y^{11}-1 = y(y^{10}+1) - (y+1)$, so

$$\gcd(y^{10}+1,\, y^{11}-1) = \gcd(y^{10}+1,\, y+1).$$

Reducing $y^{10}+1$ modulo $y+1$: since $y \equiv -1 \pmod{y+1}$,

$$y^{10}+1 \equiv (-1)^{10}+1 = 2 \pmod{y+1}.$$

Hence $d = \gcd(2,\, y+1)$. Now $51$ is odd, so $y = 51^{61}$ is odd, so $y+1$ is even. Therefore

$$d = 2.$$

### Step 2: Compute $x = \gcd(d^{671}+1,\, d^{610}-1) = \gcd(2^{671}+1,\, 2^{610}-1)$

Write $2^{671} = 2^{61}\cdot 2^{610}$, so

$$2^{671}+1 = 2^{61}(2^{610}-1) + (2^{61}+1).$$

Thus $\gcd(2^{671}+1,\, 2^{610}-1) = \gcd(2^{61}+1,\, 2^{610}-1)$.

Let $z = 2^{61}$, so $2^{610} = z^{10}$. Then

$$\gcd(z+1,\, z^{10}-1).$$

Since $z \equiv -1 \pmod{z+1}$, we get $z^{10} \equiv (-1)^{10} = 1 \pmod{z+1}$, so $z^{10}-1 \equiv 0 \pmod{z+1}$. Therefore $z+1 \mid z^{10}-1$, and

$$x = z+1 = 2^{61}+1.$$

### Step 3: Compute $X = x \bmod 10$

The powers of $2$ modulo $10$ cycle with period $4$: $2,4,8,6,2,4,\dots$

$$61 = 4\cdot 15 + 1 \implies 2^{61} \equiv 2^1 = 2 \pmod{10}.$$

Therefore

$$x = 2^{61}+1 \equiv 2+1 = 3 \pmod{10}.$$

$$\boxed{X = 3}$$

